{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Whether it be strong or whether it be weak.</b> Rashi explained: He gave them a sign, etc. And it is difficult — whence did Rashi know to say thus? If it is because it was difficult for him by what means one could know whether they are strong or not, since they did not go to do battle with them — then the difficulty applies equally to the words of the spies, who said, ״אֶפֶס כִּי עַז הָעָם הַיּוֹשֵׁב בָּאָרֶץ וְהֶעָרִים בְּצוּרוֹת״ and so forth. And since they saw the cities fortified, they should have concluded that they are weak, for against their will they must have dwelt in fortified cities — rather, surely they said this because they perceived through physical sense that the sign was nullified: that even though the cities are fortified, nevertheless the people are mighty. And whence did they know? Surely they said this because they saw them as men of great stature, giants and men of measure. And if so, perhaps ״הֶחָזָק הוּא״ is according to its plain sense. For even though he should have said ״הַמַּחֲנִים אִם מִבְצָרִים״, and from the fact that he said it with a בֵּית — ״הַבְּמַחֲנִים״ — infer from it that he was speaking of its inhabitants, in any case Rashi explained the sign upon the verse ״הֶחָזָק הוּא הֲרָפֶה״." "And it appears that the difficulty for Rashi was that he should have begun with כִּי טוֹב — why did he begin with a negative? For the Holy One, blessed be He, had already said that it is good, and it is fitting to begin each detail in accordance with the word of HaShem, as is written in all the details: הַטּוֹבָה הִיא אִם רָעָה, הַשְּׁמֵנָה הִיא אִם רָזָה, הֲיֵשׁ בָּהּ עֵץ אִם אַיִן — in all of them he began with praise, and here he began with disparagement, for ״הֶחָזָק הוּא״ — that is, it is bad for Israel and causes them harm. Therefore he explained that He gave them a sign, and each one is easy from one side and difficult from the other: for the statement ״הֶחָזָק הוּא״ is difficult for Israel from the side of ״כִּי עַז הָעָם״, yet it is good for Israel, for presumably they dwell in open cities and it is easy to come upon them. And the statement ״הֲרָפֶה״ — if it is good from the side of their weakness, in any case the matter is not good from the side that they dwell in fortified cities; and if so, they are evenly balanced. And likewise ״הַבְּמַחֲנִים אִם בְּמִבְצָרִים״ — each one is easy from one side and heavy from the other, and they are evenly balanced."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "The phrase 'סִימָן מָסַר לָהֶם' and what follows (כו׳) are an abbreviated quotation of Rashi's comment on this verse; the כו׳ ('etc.') signals omitted material."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:41:04.144090418Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}